// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 30 — CANONICAL TIME AND UTC DAY SEMANTICS
//
// Production timestamps are UTC end to end above the MT5 gateway
// (live/config.py TIME_BASE = "utc-v2"), stored as
// datetime64[us, UTC] after strategy_core.execution.prepare_candles_for_simulation,
// and every bar label is the bar OPEN (pandas resample is left-labelled,
// left-closed — verified by execution).
//
// Pine's `time` is likewise the bar-OPEN in UTC epoch milliseconds, so the two
// agree WITHOUT conversion. Everything below therefore passes "UTC" explicitly
// to the calendar functions; the chart's display timezone is irrelevant to the
// computation and must not leak into it.
//
// UTC DAY: production uses strategy_core.regime.utc_date_key — the UTC CALENDAR
// day. It does NOT use an exchange session day. This is limitation L-06, and it
// is why `timeframe.change("D")` and `request.security(..., "D", ...)` are
// forbidden here: on FX those follow a 17:00 New York session boundary and would
// roll the day at the wrong instant.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Bar-open, UTC. Identical quantity to the trace's `bar_epoch_ms`.
utcEpochMs = time

utcYear  = year(time,        "UTC")
utcMonth = month(time,       "UTC")
utcDom   = dayofmonth(time,  "UTC")
utcHour  = hour(time,        "UTC")
utcMin   = minute(time,      "UTC")

// ── LONDON WALL-CLOCK HOUR — the session classifier's input ──────────────────
//
// NOT the same quantity as `utcHour`, and the difference is the whole of
// M-SESSION-DST-1. `strategy_core/sessions.py::_london_hour` converts the UTC
// instant through `ZoneInfo("Europe/London")` and classifies on the LOCAL hour,
// so every session boundary sits one hour earlier in UTC during BST than during
// GMT. The schedule numbers (0/7/10/12/15/17) are London local and never move.
//
// Pine's `hour(time, timezone)` takes an IANA identifier and applies the same
// tz database, so this is a genuine conversion — not a month test, not a
// hard-coded +1, and not a separate summer table. Those approximations all fail
// in the ~3 weeks each spring and autumn when the UK and US clocks disagree,
// and they fail differently every year because the transition instants move.
//
// `utcHour` is deliberately kept: it is what the S1 packed export carries and
// what the trace compares, and conflating the two would make a passing
// comparison meaningless.
londonHour = hour(time, "Europe/London")

// The offset is DERIVED, never assumed — the HUD shows it so an operator can
// see GMT/BST at a glance, and a wrong timezone database would be visible
// rather than silent. Modulo 24 so the day wrap at 23:00 UTC reads as +1.
londonOffsetH = (londonHour - utcHour + 24) % 24

// Canonical day key, integer form: 2026-03-08 -> 20260308.
// Mirrors tools/oracle/session_codes.day_key_to_int so the Data Window value is
// directly comparable to the trace's day_key without arithmetic.
dayKey = utcYear * 10000 + utcMonth * 100 + utcDom

var int prevDayKey = na
dayTransition = na(prevDayKey) or dayKey != prevDayKey

// Bar completeness. Production only ever admits CLOSED bars — the bridge pulls
// closed M1 bars only (live/mt5_bridge.py). A forming bar has no production
// counterpart, so the oracle marks it and the comparator ignores it.
barClosed = barstate.isconfirmed
